 [:small {}]] [:table {:class "biodata"} [:tbody {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Date"] [:td {} "12 – 13 August 1948"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Status"] [:td {} "Olympic"]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Location"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/venues/347"} "Palace of Engineering, Wembley, London"]]] [:tr {} [:th {} "Participants"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/events/85971"} "60 from 24 countries"]]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "The hold that Hungary had over sabre competitions had loosened with the emergence of " [:a {:href "/athletes/22690"} "Aldo Montano"] ". Montano had been World Champion in both the last pre-war and first post-war championships, so the Hungarians must have uttered a sigh of relief when he was not included in the Italian team for the individual sabre event.  There was a surprise at the semi-final stage, where " [:a {:href "/athletes/22423"} "Tibor Berczelly"] ", who had excelled in the team event, was eliminated, but the other Hungarians comfortably reached the final stage."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Contemporary reports mention the sabre finals as the highlight of 1948 fencing tournament. A full 16 years after his " [:a {:href "/results/81603"} "first Olympic appearance"] ", " [:a {:href "/athletes/22451"} "Aladár Gerevich"] " won what would be the only individual title of a 28-year Olympic career. Gerevich recovered from 4-2 down to defeat teammate " [:a {:href "/athletes/22483"} "Pál Kovács"] " 5-4, and survived another close match against " [:a {:href "/athletes/22718"} "Vincenzo Pinton"] " of Italy to earn the right to be called Olympic champion.  Pinton won silver ahead of Kovács by a single hit."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Number"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {:class "bib"} "70"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/22451"} "Aladár Gerevich"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/HUN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", 